      "url": "https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/statistics-explained/index.php?title=Tertiary_education_statistics",
      "content": "# Tertiary education statistics\n\nData extracted in May 2026\n\nPlanned article update: June 2027\n\nHighlights\n----------\n\nIn the EU, there were 19.1 million tertiary education students in 2024, of which 59% were studying for bachelor’s degrees.\n\nIn 2024, 22% of tertiary education students in the EU were studying business, administration or law.\n\nWithin the EU, 58% of all tertiary education graduates in 2024 were women. In all EU countries, more women graduated from tertiary education than men.\n\nIn 2024, the most frequently awarded degrees in the EU were in the field of business, administration and law, accounting for a quarter of the total.\n\nThis article presents statistics on [tertiary education](/eurostat/statistics-explained/index.php?title=International_Standard_Classification_of_Education_(ISCED) \"International Standard Classification of Education (ISCED)\") (levels 5 to 8 of the [International Standard Classification of Education – ISCED](/eurostat/statistics-explained/index.php?title=Glossary:International_standard_classification_of_education_(ISCED) \"Glossary:International standard classification of education (ISCED)\")) in the [European Union (EU)](/eurostat/statistics-explained/index.php?title=Glossary:European_Union_(EU) \"Glossary:European Union (EU)\") and forms part of an [online publication](/eurostat/statistics-explained/index.php?title=Education_and_training_in_the_EU_-_facts_and_figures \"Education and training in the EU - facts and figures\") on [education](/eurostat/statistics-explained/index.php?title=Glossary:Education \"Glossary:Education\") and training in the EU.\n\nTertiary education – provided by universities and other tertiary educational institutions – typically follows secondary schooling. It is considered to play an essential role in society, by fostering innovation, driving economic development and growth, and more generally improving the well-being of the population. Some European universities are among the most prestigious in the world.\n\nA relatively large number of students in tertiary education are internationally mobile and study abroad: an analysis of this phenomenon is available in a [separate article](/eurostat/statistics-explained/index.php?title=Learning_mobility_statistics \"Learning mobility statistics\").\n\nParticipation by level\n----------------------\n\n[![A table showing the number of tertiary education students by sex and level of education. Data are shown for 2024 for the EU as well as EU, EFTA and enlargement countries. The complete data of the visualisation are available in the Excel file at the end of the article. For more details please use the link to the source dataset code below the image.](/eurostat/statistics-explained/images/thumb/6/67/Number_of_tertiary_education_students_by_sex_and_level_of_education%2C_2024_%281_000%29_ET2026.png/700px-Number_of_tertiary_education_students_by_sex_and_level_of_education%2C_2024_%281_000%29_ET2026.png)](/eurostat/statistics-explained/index.php?title=File:Number_of_tertiary_education_students_by_sex_and_level_of_education,_2024_(1_000)_ET2026.png)\n\nTable 1: Number of tertiary education students by sex and level of education, 2024  \n*Source:* Eurostat (online data code: [educ\\_uoe\\_enrt01](https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/databrowser/bookmark/7c355076-fa4b-41b6-9227-aa4e88b0532b?lang=en))\n\nTable 1 presents data on the number of students in each of 4 [levels of tertiary education](/eurostat/statistics-explained/index.php?title=Glossary:International_standard_classification_of_education_(ISCED) \"Glossary:International standard classification of education (ISCED)\"). Bachelor’s, master’s and doctoral levels of tertiary education are found in all EU countries, while short-cycle tertiary education, which is typically vocational (occupationally specific) and designed to prepare students for the labour market, is not part of the education system in Bulgaria, Estonia, Greece, Romania or Finland. In several other EU countries – namely Czechia, Germany, Croatia, Lithuania and Poland – short-cycle tertiary education accounts for less than 1.0% of the total number of tertiary students.\n\nIn the [EU](/eurostat/statistics-explained/index.php?title=Glossary:EU_enlargements \"Glossary:EU enlargements\"), there were 19.1 million tertiary education students in 2024 (see Table 1). Germany – the most populous country in the EU – had 3.3 million tertiary education students, which was the highest number in the EU and equivalent to 17.2% of the EU total. France (15.1% of the total), Spain (12.8%) and Italy (12.1%) had the next largest shares of the EU’s tertiary student population.\n\nAmong the 19.1 million tertiary education students in the EU\n\n* 7.9% were following short-cycle tertiary courses\n* 59.0% were studying for bachelor’s degrees\n* 29.4% were studying for master’s degrees\n* 3.8% were studying for doctoral degrees.\n\nStudents of bachelor’s and master’s degrees accounted for 88.3% of the total number of tertiary students in the EU in 2024. The highest shares for these 2 levels of education were reported for Poland (98.5%), Bulgaria, Lithuania (both 97.4%), Croatia (96.9%), Italy (96.3%), Greece (96.1%) and Romania (96.0%). Notably lower shares were recorded for France (80.5%), Austria (79.1%), Latvia (78.3%), Ireland (77.9%), Luxembourg (77.5%) and – most notably – Spain (70.6%).\n\nShort-cycle tertiary courses were most common in Spain where they accounted for 25.3% of all tertiary students; they were also relatively common in Ireland, Latvia, France and Austria.\n\nLuxembourg, Cyprus, Austria and France were the only EU countries where fewer than 50% of all tertiary students were studying for bachelor’s degrees. Among EU countries, the highest share of tertiary students who were studying for bachelor’s degrees was 84.0% in Greece.\n\nGreece had the lowest share of tertiary students studying for master’s degrees in 2024, around an eighth (12.1%) of all tertiary students. This share was also below 20.0% in Ireland and Spain. By contrast, the highest shares were recorded in Cyprus (48.6%) and Croatia (40.5%).\n\nBy far, the highest share of tertiary students studying for doctoral degrees in 2024 among the EU countries was recorded in Luxembourg (12.6%), with the next highest shares in Germany (6.3%) and Portugal (6.0%). In line with the fact that Germany had the highest total number of tertiary students among the EU countries, it also had, by far, the highest number of students studying for doctoral degrees (204 900) – see Table 1. This was more than twice the number of students studying for a doctoral degree in any of the other EU countries; the next highest counts were recorded in Spain (99 400) and France (69 600). Although France had the second highest total number of tertiary students among the EU countries, at 2.4% the share of students studying for doctoral degrees in France was the third lowest among the EU countries. The lowest shares of doctoral students in the total number of tertiary education students were observed in Poland (1.5%) and Italy (2.1%).\n\nParticipation in tertiary education by sex\n------------------------------------------\n\nIn 2024, women accounted for 54.9% of all tertiary students in the EU. The share of women among tertiary students was\n\n* 48.5% for those following short-cycle tertiary courses\n* 54.3% for those studying for bachelor’s degrees\n* 58.6% for those studying for master’s degrees\n* 49.7% for those studying for doctoral degrees.\n\nAs such, the majority of short-cycle tertiary and doctoral students were male, while relatively large majorities of bachelor’s and master’s students were female.\n\nIn 2024, 60.7% of all tertiary students in Sweden were female, while the share was close to this level in Poland (60.1%) and Estonia (59.9%). Women were also in a majority among tertiary students in all of the other EU countries except for Greece (49.4% were female) and Ireland (49.0%).\n\n* For short-cycle courses, 10 of the 22 EU countries in which such courses exist had more female tha"
